\A{folderstore} The folder store

The folders and files of the KiTTY++ folder store, for administrators
who generate session files, keep them in Git or inspect them.

The folder store is in use when \c{kitty.ini} holds
\c{[KiTTY] savemode=dir}, the default of \c{kitty_portable.exe}, which
writes that key on its first start. Otherwise the settings are in the
registry under \c{HKEY_CURRENT_USER\\Software\\kapper.net\\KiTTY}.
klink, kscp and ksftp from the same folder read the same store. The
settings below are on the Storage & Backup panel, \k{kitty-cfg-storage}.

\H{folderstore-where} Where the store is

\c{kitty.ini} is the first of these that exists: the file
\c{%KITTY_INI_FILE%} points to, \c{kitty.ini} in the folder of the
program, \c{%APPDATA%\\KiTTY\\kitty.ini} (not looked at by the portable
build). The KiTTY.ini leaf shows the file in use,
\k{kitty-cfg-ini-view}. Every key below is in its \c{[KiTTY]} section.
klink, kscp, ksftp and kageant also take the folder store when
\c{kitty.ini} has no \c{savemode} key but a \c{Sessions} folder or a
\c{KiTTYState} file is beside it.

\b \e{Configuration directory}: \c{configdir}, an absolute path or a
path below the folder of the program. Not set, or not existing: the
folder of the program.

\b \e{Session folder}: \c{sessions}, Windows variables such as
\c{%USERPROFILE%} expanded. \c{X:\\...} is absolute; every other value,
one starting with \c{\\} included, is below the folder of the program,
so a UNC path does not work. Not set: \c{Sessions} in the configuration
directory. kitty.exe creates a missing session folder.

\b \e{Store root}: the parent of the session folder when its last
component is \c{Sessions} (any case), else the session folder itself.

\b \e{Host-key folder}: \c{sshhostkeys}, resolved like \c{sessions}.
Not set: \c{SshHostKeys} in the store root.

\H{folderstore-layout} The layout

By default store root and configuration directory are one folder:

\c <configuration directory>\
\c     kitty.ini        global options, when it lives here
\c     KiTTYState       remembered state
\c     Jumplist         recent sessions
\c     PUTTY.RND        random seed
\c     Sessions\        session files, folders as directories
\c     SshHostKeys\     accepted host keys
\c     SshHostCAs\      trusted host certificate authorities
\c     Security\        master password check material
\c     Proxies\         named proxy definitions
\c     Launcher\        the launcher's menu
\c     Commands\        User Command menu entries
\c     Folders\         the same, per folder
\c     Sessions_Commands\  the same, per session
\c     Backups\         dated copies of the directory
\c     TermEnvHint\     one mark per host
\c     WindowPos\       terminal window positions

When \c{sessions} points elsewhere, \c{Proxies}, \c{Launcher},
\c{Commands}, \c{Folders}, \c{Sessions_Commands} and \c{Backups} stay in
the configuration directory; \c{KiTTYState}, \c{Jumplist},
\c{PUTTY.RND}, \c{SshHostKeys} (unless \c{sshhostkeys} is set),
\c{SshHostCAs}, \c{Security}, \c{TermEnvHint} and \c{WindowPos} are in
the store root. Files of the store
root are never listed as sessions; a tool that regenerates the session
folder must leave them in place.

Host-key files and the files in \c{Security}, \c{TermEnvHint} and
\c{WindowPos} are written to a temporary file and moved over the old
one. Session files, \c{KiTTYState}, \c{Jumplist} and the files in
\c{Proxies} and \c{SshHostCAs} are written over in place.

With \c{readonly=yes} no backup is written and a missing session folder
is not created.

\H{folderstore-sessions} Session files and folders

\b One file per session: \c{Linux\\web\\srv01} is the file
\c{Linux\\web\\srv01} below the session folder, plus the session file
extension. Each directory is one folder level, up to 24 deep.

\b A directory without a session file is not listed. Deleting or moving
a session removes the directories this leaves empty, up to the session
folder; one that still holds any file (a \c{.gitkeep}) stays.

\b Names starting with a dot (\c{.git}, \c{.gitkeep}) are skipped.
Junctions and symbolic links to directories are not followed. A file
with a reparse point (a placeholder of a synced folder) is read.

\b A session file has a line starting with \c{HostName} or
\c{Protocol} directly followed by \c{=} or \c{\\}: case-sensitive, no
space before it, no byte-order mark before the first line. Other files
(\c{README.md}, a log) are not listed.

\b Names are in the ANSI code page. A file or folder whose name does not
fit it is marked in the session list, with a rename offered; such a
folder is not looked into.

\b \c{fileextension} (\q{Session File Extension}): none by default, a
dot put in front when missing; ends every session file name and is not
shown in the list. A value holding a control character or one of
\c{\\ / : * ? " < > |} is not used.

\b The directory decides the folder; a \c{Folder} value in a file inside
a directory is ignored and corrected by the next save. See
\k{kitty-cfg-folders}.

\S{folderstore-escape} Name escaping

Each path component is escaped on its own:

\b \c{% \\ / : * ? " < > |}, control characters and DEL become \c{%}
and two uppercase hex digits: \c{core/sw01} is \c{core%2Fsw01}.

\b A leading or trailing dot becomes \c{%2E}, a trailing space \c{%20}.

\b A device name (\c{CON}, \c{PRN}, \c{AUX}, \c{NUL}, \c{COM1}-\c{COM9},
\c{LPT1}-\c{LPT9}), also with an extension, has its first character
escaped: \c{%43ON}, \c{%63on.txt}.

\b Everything else stays: inner spaces, \c{=}, \c{#}, bytes above 127.
\c{Default Settings} is the file \c{Default Settings}.

Reading decodes \c{%} and two hex digits in either case; any other
\c{%} stays. A file spelt another way (\c{%20} for a space) is listed
under its decoded name and saved under its own file name. When two
files decode to one session, the others are marked in the list.

Also read: a file without the session file extension (its next save
adds it and removes the old file; with both files present, a save is
refused), and at the top level a file holding an escaped \c{\\}
(\c{Linux%5Cweb01}, the session \c{Linux\\web01}), moved to
\c{Linux\\web01} by its next save; below the top level such a name is
not listed. Without an extension, a save, move
or new folder that needs the name of an existing session or folder is
refused.

\H{folderstore-format} The session file format

Text, one setting per line, in either form:

\c HostName=web01.example.com
\c HostName\web01.example.com\

The first is KiTTY++'s, the second old KiTTY's (portable store and
\c{.ktx}).

\b The first \c{=} or \c{\\} ends the key, so a value may hold \c{=}.
In the second form one trailing \c{\\} is dropped; \c{Key\\0} also reads.

\b A line with neither \c{=} nor \c{\\} (a comment, a blank line) is
kept in place through every save.

\b Keys are case-sensitive, nothing is trimmed: \c{HostName = x} is the
key \c{HostName} followed by a space, with a value starting with a space.

\b Values escape control characters, DEL and \c{% \\ / : * ? " < > |}
as \c{%} and two uppercase hex digits: \c{PublicKeyFile=D:%5Cid.ppk},
a line break \c{%0A}. Reading decodes either case.

\b Numbers and switches are decimal: \c{PortNumber=2222},
\c{AgentFwd=1}.

\b A repeated key takes the later value; a save keeps one line, at the
place of the first.

\b LF or CRLF, kept per line; an added line takes the ending of the
first line, a new file LF. Lines have no length limit. A byte-order
mark becomes part of the first key.

A save reads the file and writes it whole. Unchanged lines are written
back as read; a changed value is replaced in place as \c{Key=value}; new
keys go to the end (every setting KiTTY++ has). Retired keys are
removed: \c{SaveWindowPos}, \c{OSC52WarnBeforeClipboardSync},
\c{CheckUpdateStartup}, \c{ScriptFileName}, \c{Present},
\c{DisableBottomButtons}, \c{Notes}.

\H{folderstore-passwords} Passwords

\c{Password} and \c{ProxyPassword} start with a marker, in a file
escaped as \c{MPW2%3A}, \c{DPAPI1%3A}; both spellings are read.

\b \c{MPW2:<salt>.<payload>}: master password, self-contained (base64
Argon2id salt, a dot, AES-256-CBC with HMAC). Opens on any PC with the
master password alone.

\b \c{MPW1:<payload>}: master password, salt in the store's
\c{Security\\MasterPwSalt}. Read, no longer written.

\b \c{DPAPI1:<base64>}: Windows DPAPI, this account on this PC only.

\b \c{PLAIN:<password>}: clear text for provisioning scripts; never
written by KiTTY++.

\b No marker: clear text. In a file with a \c{Key\\value\\} line, a
\c{Password} in old KiTTY's encrypted form is decoded.

\c{PortablePasswordProtection} (\q{Passwords protected with}) sets what
a save writes: \c{master} (default) \c{MPW2}, the first save setting up
the master password (\c{-masterpwfile} for scripts,
\k{using-cmdline-masterpwfile}); \c{dpapi} \c{DPAPI1}; \c{legacy} clear
text. Reading goes by marker. A save keeps the stored line when the
password is unchanged and already in the target form, when the value
cannot be opened here, or when the master password is locked (reported).
Replacing an unmarked or \c{PLAIN:} value prompts first;
\c{WarnLegacyPasswordUpgrade=no} skips the prompt. klink, kscp and
ksftp keep the line.

\H{folderstore-security} Security

Written once, when a master password is first set up:
\c{Security\\MasterPwSalt} (base64 Argon2id salt) and
\c{Security\\MasterPwVerifier} (a fixed text in \c{MPW1:} form). Neither
holds a password or the key.

\b With \c{MasterPwVerifier} present and the policy \c{master}, the
configuration window and the launcher prompt for the master password at
start. Otherwise the prompt comes when an \c{MPW2} value is read.

\b Without \c{Security}, \c{MPW2} values open (each carries its salt);
\c{MPW1} values do not. The next protected save sets up a new master
password; older \c{MPW2} values still need the one they were written
with.

\b A \c{Security} folder copied from another store makes that master
password the one checked at start and used for new values. To share one
master password between portable copies, copy one \c{Security} folder
into each before saving passwords.

\b A store with master-password values, no \c{MasterPwSalt} and a salt
still in the registry gets salt and verifier copied from the registry
at start, once.

\H{folderstore-proxies} Proxies

\c{Proxies} in the configuration directory holds one file per named
proxy (\k{kitty-cfg-named-proxies}), made by the first save. File name:
the definition's name with space, \c{\\ * ? : / " < > | %}, control
characters, bytes above 126 and a leading dot as \c{%} and two
uppercase hex digits. \c{None} and \c{Default} are ignored. Both line
forms are read; a save works as for a session file but writes changed
and new lines as \c{Key\\value\\}. The import of an export reads only
\c{Key\\value\\} lines.

\b \c{ProxyMethod}: \c{0} none, \c{1} SOCKS 4, \c{2} SOCKS 5, \c{3}
HTTP, \c{4} Telnet, \c{5} local command, \c{6} SSH port forwarding,
\c{7} SSH command, \c{8} SSH subsystem; other numbers none.

\b \c{ProxyHost}, \c{ProxyPort}, \c{ProxyUsername},
\c{ProxyExcludeList}, \c{ProxyTelnetCommand}.

\b \c{ProxyPassword}: as a session password, \k{folderstore-passwords}.

\b \c{ProxyDNS}: \c{0} no, \c{1} auto, \c{2} yes.
\c{ProxyLocalhost}: \c{1} proxies \c{localhost} too.
\c{ProxyLogToTerm}: \c{0} on, \c{1} off, \c{2} auto.
\c{ProxyHostIs}: \c{0} or \c{1}; absent means the global default.

A session selects one with \c{ProxySelection=<name>}.
\c{- Session defined proxy -} (default) uses the session's own proxy
settings, \c{- No proxy -} none (\k{kitty-cfg-proxy-override}). An
unknown name falls back to the session's own settings, else none.

\H{folderstore-hostkeys} SshHostKeys and SshHostCAs

One file per host key, named \c{<key type>@<port>:<host>}: the host
escaped as in PuTTY's registry (space, \c{\\ * ? %}, control
characters, bytes above 126, a leading dot), then the whole name escaped
as a session value (\c{:} is \c{%3A}), then \c{hostkeyextension}
appended. The file holds PuTTY's text form of the key; it matches only
when identical to the key presented. Two more files, \c{%3Afirst}
(written once) and \c{%3Awhen} (each store), hold a local time
\c{YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ss} for the Host keys panel
(\k{kitty-cfg-host-keys}); deleting a key there deletes all three.

\c ssh-ed25519@22%3Aweb01.example.com
\c ssh-ed25519@22%3Aweb01.example.com%3Afirst
\c ssh-ed25519@22%3Aweb01.example.com%3Awhen

\c{hostkeyextension} (\q{Host Key File Extension}): none by default, a
dot put in front when missing. A file without it is renamed at its
first lookup; when both exist, the one with it is read.
\c{hostkeyextensionapplied} records the ending last applied.

\c{SshHostCAs} holds one file per CA (\k{kitty-cfg-host-cas}), named as
a session value, written anew on each save, with \c{Key=value} lines
\c{PublicKey} (base64), \c{Validity} (percent-encoded host expression),
\c{PermitRSASHA1}, \c{PermitRSASHA256}, \c{PermitRSASHA512} (\c{0}/\c{1}).

\H{folderstore-state} State files

\c{KiTTYState}, in session file format, read at each use:
\c{LastSession}, \c{LastFolder} (\c{Default} is the root),
\c{ManageFolder}, \c{ManageSession}, \c{ManageTag},
\c{ShowForeignSessions} (\c{1} lists the old KiTTY and PuTTY registry
sessions), \c{ForeignSessionsNotice} (bits: \c{1} at start, \c{2} under
the list), \c{SessionArrangeOffered}, \c{UpdateLatest},
\c{UpdateLatestBeta}. A missing file means defaults.

\b \c{Jumplist}: one escaped session name per line, newest first;
names of deleted sessions are dropped on change.

\b \c{PUTTY.RND}: binary random seed.

\b \c{TermEnvHint}: one file per host holding \c{1}, marking that the
\c{COLORTERM}/\c{TERM_PROGRAM} notice was shown.

\b \c{WindowPos}: one file per monitor layout.

\b \c{Launcher}: one file per entry, folders as directories, rewritten
from the session list.

\H{folderstore-backups} Backups

\c{Backups\\kitty-portable-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS} (local time) and
\c{Backups\\kitty-portable-latest} (replaced each time) in the
configuration directory. \c{portablebackupcount}: dated copies kept,
default 5, at most 50, \c{0} off. A copy holds \c{kitty.ini} and the
configuration directory, without \c{Backups} and top-level \c{.exe},
\c{.dll}, \c{.log}, \c{.dmp} files; a session or host-key folder outside
it is not included. A failed copy is deleted. No backup with
\c{conf=no} or \c{readonly=yes}.

Written when the configuration window is committed (\q{Open},
\q{Change Settings}) after a store change, before it overwrites or
deletes a session, and on \c{/savereg}. To restore, close every KiTTY++
program and copy a backup back into the configuration directory.

\H{folderstore-edits} Editing while KiTTY++ runs

Nothing watches the store. A session file is read when the session
loads (\c{-load} always reads the file); the configuration window reads
the list when it opens, kageant when its tray menu opens. Host-key files
are read at each connection, a named proxy when a session using it
connects, \c{Security} when a process first needs the master password,
\c{kitty.ini} at start.
KiTTY++ writes session files, \c{KiTTYState} and proxy files over in
place from what it read a moment before: an edit made in between is
lost.

\H{folderstore-ktx} .ktx export files

Application > Migration exports (\k{kitty-cfg-migration}),
\c{-exportall} writes and \c{-importdir} reads one flat file per
session: the whole name escaped as one component (\c{Linux%5Cweb01.ktx}),
ending in the session file extension or \c{.ktx}. Default Settings is
not exported. Lines are \c{Key\\value\\}; reading takes both forms.
Passwords are \c{MPW2} under the export password (five characters at
least), or DPAPI with \q{this PC only}; no master password is set up.
Named proxies go into a \c{Proxies} folder beside the files. An import
saves each as an ordinary session, protecting passwords for the
destination store, \c{PLAIN:} included. A line that is not empty, does
not end in \c{\\} and has no \c{=} before any \c{\\} marks the older
encrypted form, which is then decoded. \q{Make a portable copy}
uses the export but writes \c{Key=value} lines with LF.
